There has been quite the tug of war in VTK w.r.t. using a forwarder between
vtkGLTFDocumentLoader and vtkGLTFImporter. The history is as follows:

 - Originally, the loader had forwarded its events to the importer.
 - in de1602836934 (MR#12033) (2025-03-30) it was removed as part of a bug fix.
 - In 55ef1381 (2025-09-10) it was restored, having been flagged as an
   accidental removal.
 - In 571196de4efff (2026-05-29) it was reverted referencing MR#12033.
 - In 2d35cc4aaf230 (2026-06-23) it was restored again (possibly mistakenly).

Drake, however, depends on loader events being forwarded to the importer (where
Drake has a registered observer).

I broached this topic in VTK MR#12033 (see the link):

https://gitlab.kitware.com/vtk/vtk/-/merge_requests/12033#note_1827292

Characterization of the problem:

  - The forwarder doesn't do what it promises.
  - Once an event is forwarded to *any* observer, it must be resolved by an
    observer, or not at all.
  - For any VTK user that doesn't provide an observer, all GLTF loader messages
    simply evaporated.
  - This is why forwarding was considered "defective".

Proposed solution (not documented in the linked VTK MR above):

  - The mechanism relying on the existence of an observer needs to know if there
    is *ultimately* an observer (and not just a forwarder to a dead end).
  - So, vtkCommand can report its forwarding target (defaulting to null).
  - vtkEventForwarderCommand reports its actual target.
  - vtkObject gets a new API: HasEffectiveObserver() which can recursively chase
    the forwarding chain to determine if there is an effective observer.
  - Error/warning handler only relies on InvokeEvent() if there's an *effective*
    observer.

Conclusion:

  - This solution has not been proposed to VTK. Perhaps it can be upstreamed.

Vaguely changes to vtkGLTFDocumentLoader.cxx:

  - If VTK encounters a glTF extension it doesn't support, it warns that the
    extension will be ignored.
  - However, if it encounters an *image* with an unexpected mime type, it
    dispatches a warning.
  - We simply disable the warning in this patch as an approximation of the
    correct solution.
  - Justification:
    - Generally, we expect the unsupported mime type to be related to an
      unsupported glTF extension. A warning for that has already been
      dispatched and now we are "ignoring" the result as noted in that warning.
    - It won't fail silently -- if an image is used but couldn't be loaded, an
      error will be dispatched. If it isn't used, ignoring it is harmless.
    - Even if the unsupported image mime type is unrelated to an unsupported
      extension, we still get the used/unused behavior as described above.
    - Drake *attempts* to silence warnings due to KHR_texture_basisu (see
      RenderEngineVtkParams::gltf_extensions). However, it operates purely on
      warnings about the glTF extensions and cannot easily be extended to handle
      these redundant per-image warnings.
  - Counter argument:
    - The most proper thing to do is to distinguish between the two cases and
      provide warnings for unsupported mime types that *aren't* related to
      unsupported extensions.


